A crawler that never stops on its own is a crawler that will eventually spend your whole IP reputation on a bad hour.
I run a daily collector over public channel preview pages (no login, just the web view). One Tuesday it woke up, got a wall of clean 200s with structurally empty bodies, and kept marching through the queue for forty minutes. Nothing threw. Nothing logged an error. It just politely walked off the cliff, one polite request at a time.
That run taught me the rule I now enforce everywhere: a fetch loop needs a trip condition, not just a try/except.
class Breaker:
    def __init__(self, threshold=3, cooldown=600):
        self.threshold = threshold
        self.fail = 0
        self.cooldown = cooldown
        self.opened_at = None

    def record(self, ok):
        self.fail = 0 if ok else self.fail + 1
        if self.fail >= self.threshold:
            self.opened_at = time.time()

    @property
    def open(self):
        return (
            self.opened_at is not None
            and time.time() - self.opened_at < self.cooldown
        )

Three consecutive failures: the breaker opens, the run aborts, and the next attempt is deferred by a cooldown. Closed → open → half-open is the same state machine a payment gateway uses, and it maps cleanly onto collection runs.
The breaker above is useless if ok means "no exception." Twelve months of logs convinced me that a run is only healthy when all three of these are true:
Status is 2xx — the cheap check everyone has.
Body has content — non-empty, above a sane byte floor. A 200 with a 900-byte consent shell is a failure.
Expected structure is present — for me: at least one message element in the preview markup. If the page shape my parser was written for isn't in the response, the response failed, full stop.
Score all three per page, feed the score into the breaker, and the "empty 200" cliff becomes a trip on page three, not a forty-minute walk.
